## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical, beginner-friendly observability implementation: instrumenting a new service end-to-end with minimal changes and validating traces in a production UI.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry auto-instrumentation
- Trace-context propagation and log correlation
- Lightweight instrumentation for HTTP and database spans
- OTLP exporter configuration and backend dashboard validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Node example: enable auto-instrumentation
// Install: @opentelemetry/api @opentelemetry/sdk-node @opentelemetry/instrumentation-http
import { NodeSDK } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-node';
import { getNodeAutoInstrumentations } from '@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node';

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations()],
  serviceName: 'accounts-service',
  traceExporter: new OTLPTraceExporter()
});
sdk.start();

// Trace ID in logs
import { trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const traceId = trace.getActiveSpan()?.spanContext().traceId;
  console.log(`Request ${req.method} ${req.path} [trace=${traceId}]`);
  next();
});
```

## Validation Plan
- Configure OTLP exporter to your backend (Jaeger/Tempo/Grafana)
- Verify HTTP request spans appear in dashboard
- Confirm database query spans are captured
- Check trace IDs appear in structured logs
- Test end-to-end with synthetic request and validate trace flow

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates a candidate's ability to design safe, scalable chaos experiments for multi-region, real-time systems with precise blast radius control and explicit recovery criteria. Strong answers demonstrate experience with distributed systems resilience patterns and operational maturity.

## Key Concepts

- Chaos experiments with blast radius and safety constraints
- Multi-region latency, replication lag, and data integrity
- Circuit breakers, backpressure, and local message buffering
- Canary deployments with automated rollback criteria
- Telemetry: RED metrics (Rate, Errors, Duration), p95 latency thresholds, error budgets, and distributed tracing

## Code Example

```javascript
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold, timeout) {
    this.failures = 0;
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.timeout = timeout;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
    this.lastFailure = null;
  }

  recordFailure() {
    this.failures++;
    this.lastFailure = Date.now();
    if (this.failures >= this.threshold) {
      this.state = 'OPEN';
      setTimeout(() => this.state = 'HALF-OPEN', this.timeout);
    }
  }

  recordSuccess() {
    this.failures = 0;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
  }

  canExecute() {
    return this.state !== 'OPEN';
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical incident response for streaming workloads with backpressure, not just static services.

## Key Concepts

- Lag metrics per partition and end-to-end tracing
- Alert thresholds for latency and lag
- Backpressure and scaling strategies (max_poll_records, HPA, rate limiting)
- Canary rollout and rollback with validation

## Code Example

